{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfC5ZQn2xvndq7BcTzi9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PiYdtG8KjrwzPWnJZKToeB","name":"WebFetch","input":{"url":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub","prompt":"List the files in this directory and summarize how a game integrates with this hub for multiplayer and for the run record / exit interview: the APIs, message formats, required files, and any README instructions verbatim where important."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7220,"cache_read_input_tokens":17706,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7220,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0729cc04-0deb-412e-a406-b462eee591a2","uuid":"b5e855be-c84a-43d6-83e3-227e62f73c9c","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:49.238Z","request_id":"req_011CgfC5Z3T4wHrvyuJyUot6","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01PiYdtG8KjrwzPWnJZKToeB":{"url":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub","prompt":"List the files in this directory and summarize how a game integrates with this hub for multiplayer and for the run record / exit interview: the APIs, message formats, required files, and any README instructions verbatim where important."}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PiYdtG8KjrwzPWnJZKToeB","type":"tool_result","content":"**Directory listing (as shown on the page):** `examples/` (folder), `README.md`, `bridge.js`, `harness.html`, `retrofit.md`, `round.js`, `session.js`. The page says \"View all files,\" so this list may be incomplete.\n\n**Run record / exit interview:** The page doesn't mention either one. The closest features are the `end` event and the `stats` and `awards` fields on results, which are shown with the round's results. I can't say more without the source for those.\n\n## Multiplayer integration\n\n**Required files and setup**\n- `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. The hub replaces unmodified copies with new versions, but not edited ones.\n- Required `<meta>` tags: `hub:session` (value `1`), `hub:session-players` (`MIN-MAX`), `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal`, and `hub:session-howto`. Optional: `hub:session-minutes` and `hub:webgpu`.\n- For local testing, serve `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` next to the game, then run it with `python3 -m http.server 8000`.\n\n**Modes**\n- `parallel`: each copy plays its own round and sends nothing.\n- `ghost`: each copy also sends its player's position and actions via `send()`.\n- `shared-world`: the host's copy runs one world. Other copies send their inputs to the host and render the world the host sends.\n\n**Lifecycle calls and events**\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats })` returns a promise of `info`, or `null` if there is no hub.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the round can start.\n- `AIHub.time()` gives the shared timeline. It is negative during the countdown and `-Infinity` before `start`.\n- Events: `start`, `message`, `leave`, `time-up`, `end`.\n\n**Reporting**\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` (parallel and ghost).\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` (parallel and ghost, once).\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })` (shared world, host only, once).\n\n**Messaging**\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends JSON up to 16 KB. Reliable is the default.\n- Limits: about 20 messages per second per copy, with bursts up to 40.\n- Use `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` to receive.\n- `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on every copy. In parallel and ghost, `Math.random` is seeded automatically.\n\n**End reasons:** `finished`, `void`, `host-left`, `sat-out`, `session-ended`.\n\n**Rules that matter:** no network traffic outside the hub, no player-to-player chat or custom names, and no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, popups, or navigating away during a session.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe hub runs the same checks when a game joins the library. `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes `\"true\"` at the end, and the game passes when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. The repo's `scripts/hub-check.mjs` runs these checks with Playwright."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0729cc04-0deb-412e-a406-b462eee591a2","uuid":"a3e08166-0ba4-4617-81e7-ec91ad7adf78","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:57.828Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":287995,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Directory listing (as shown on the page):** `examples/` (folder), `README.md`, `bridge.js`, `harness.html`, `retrofit.md`, `round.js`, `session.js`. The page says \"View all files,\" so this list may be incomplete.\n\n**Run record / exit interview:** The page doesn't mention either one. The closest features are the `end` event and the `stats` and `awards` fields on results, which are shown with the round's results. I can't say more without the source for those.\n\n## Multiplayer integration\n\n**Required files and setup**\n- `session.js` is pasted unmodified as the first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`. The hub replaces unmodified copies with new versions, but not edited ones.\n- Required `<meta>` tags: `hub:session` (value `1`), `hub:session-players` (`MIN-MAX`), `hub:session-mode`, `hub:session-goal`, and `hub:session-howto`. Optional: `hub:session-minutes` and `hub:webgpu`.\n- For local testing, serve `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, and `round.js` next to the game, then run it with `python3 -m http.server 8000`.\n\n**Modes**\n- `parallel`: each copy plays its own round and sends nothing.\n- `ghost`: each copy also sends its player's position and actions via `send()`.\n- `shared-world`: the host's copy runs one world. Other copies send their inputs to the host and render the world the host sends.\n\n**Lifecycle calls and events**\n- `AIHub.connect({ mode, seats })` returns a promise of `info`, or `null` if there is no hub.\n- `AIHub.ready()` is called once, when the round can start.\n- `AIHub.time()` gives the shared timeline. It is negative during the countdown and `-Infinity` before `start`.\n- Events: `start`, `message`, `leave`, `time-up`, `end`.\n\n**Reporting**\n- `AIHub.progress({ score, label })` (parallel and ghost).\n- `AIHub.result({ time })` or `AIHub.result({ score })` (parallel and ghost, once).\n- `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, won })` (shared world, host only, once).\n\n**Messaging**\n- `AIHub.send(data, { to, reliable })` sends JSON up to 16 KB. Reliable is the default.\n- Limits: about 20 messages per second per copy, with bursts up to 40.\n- Use `AIHub.on('message', (data, from) => …)` to receive.\n- `AIHub.rng(label)` gives the same numbers on every copy. In parallel and ghost, `Math.random` is seeded automatically.\n\n**End reasons:** `finished`, `void`, `host-left`, `sat-out`, `session-ended`.\n\n**Rules that matter:** no network traffic outside the hub, no player-to-player chat or custom names, and no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, popups, or navigating away during a session.\n\n## Testing\n\nThe hub runs the same checks when a game joins the library. `document.body.dataset.complete` becomes `\"true\"` at the end, and the game passes when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true. The repo's `scripts/hub-check.mjs` runs these checks with Playwright.","durationMs":8587,"url":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01PiYdtG8KjrwzPWnJZKToeB","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015vEC7dfPjT4tdeAnfxybaX","type":"tool_result","content":"## Files in `dist/handoff/`\n\nThe page shows these entries. It says \"View all files,\" so the listing may be incomplete.\n\n- **`docs/`**: Asset documentation, including `docs/ASSETS.md`, which covers still objects and materials.\n- **`game/`**: A template game (`index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs`) that climbs, has a guard with sight, an HTML HUD, and scenes.\n- **`kits/`**: Readable 2D genre kits. Each has a game (`<id>.js`), a playable page (`<id>.html`), and a headless test (`test.mjs`), plus a `README.md` table.\n- **`modules/`**: Engine modules that load after the agent edition, if present.\n- **`src/assets/`**: An asset store of objects, materials, and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, with catalogs.\n- **`tools/`**: `check.mjs` (headless browser play and error reporting), `filmstrip.mjs` (frame recording), `replay.mjs` (session replay), and asset tools (`asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`).\n- **`API.md`**: The API card. It is the page to read in full.\n- **`API-INDEX.md`**: A one-line-per-name index of public engine names, with signatures, units, and defaults.\n- **`AI_GUIDE.md`**: Recipes by genre, the target look, frame order, heights, and recorded sessions.\n- **`README.md`**: The handoff instructions.\n- **`index.html`**: A playable page.\n- **`my-3d2dge.js`**: The full engine, in one readable file with no dependencies.\n- **`my-3d2dge-agent.js`**: The smaller \"essential\" engine edition, with its own shorter manual.\n- **`package.json`**: Pins Playwright for the tools.\n\n## What the handoff expects\n\n- **Start from a kit or the template.** The README says: \"Start from a game, not an empty file.\"\n- **Read `API.md` first**, then search `API-INDEX.md` by name (for example, `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`).\n- **Don't build standard features yourself.** The README says not to build \"a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen.\" Configure them with `new E.Game({ menu: ... })`, and put heavy setup in `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n- **Objects:** Read `docs/ASSETS.md` before drawing objects. For example, `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a core-library object in every view.\n- **Asset store:** A game loads a set with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`.\n\n## Bundling into a single HTML file\n\nThe README says: \"To hand a single file to a person, inline the two scripts into the page.\" It doesn't give further steps.\n\n## Run and test commands (verbatim from the README)\n\n```\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\n```\n\nThe README also says: \"Open `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`).\"\n\n## Headless testing\n\n`new E.Game()` runs headless in Node. `game.advance(seconds)` runs fixed 1/120 s steps, and `game.input.tap('jump')` simulates input. The README says the same inputs produce the same game on every machine.\n\n## Native perspectives\n\nThe README mentions views but doesn't list them. It says the docs define what x, y, and z mean \"in each view,\" and that 3D games are built on the 2D engine's views. The 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) is not included here.\n\n## Not covered in the content\n\n- **Art style:** The README only mentions \"the look to aim for,\" which is covered in `AI_GUIDE.md`. It doesn't describe the style.\n- **Animation:** Not described.\n- **Audio:** Only mentioned in passing as part of the kits (\"sound, touch controls\"). No audio system is described."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0729cc04-0deb-412e-a406-b462eee591a2","uuid":"185069fd-1d87-4174-b3af-0d4c13802473","timestamp":"2026-11-03T02:17:58.602Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":234269,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"## Files in `dist/handoff/`\n\nThe page shows these entries. It says \"View all files,\" so the listing may be incomplete.\n\n- **`docs/`**: Asset documentation, including `docs/ASSETS.md`, which covers still objects and materials.\n- **`game/`**: A template game (`index.html`, `game.js`, `test.mjs`) that climbs, has a guard with sight, an HTML HUD, and scenes.\n- **`kits/`**: Readable 2D genre kits. Each has a game (`<id>.js`), a playable page (`<id>.html`), and a headless test (`test.mjs`), plus a `README.md` table.\n- **`modules/`**: Engine modules that load after the agent edition, if present.\n- **`src/assets/`**: An asset store of objects, materials, and sprites converted from CC0 libraries, with catalogs.\n- **`tools/`**: `check.mjs` (headless browser play and error reporting), `filmstrip.mjs` (frame recording), `replay.mjs` (session replay), and asset tools (`asset-find.mjs`, `asset-sheet.mjs`, `asset-set.mjs`).\n- **`API.md`**: The API card. It is the page to read in full.\n- **`API-INDEX.md`**: A one-line-per-name index of public engine names, with signatures, units, and defaults.\n- **`AI_GUIDE.md`**: Recipes by genre, the target look, frame order, heights, and recorded sessions.\n- **`README.md`**: The handoff instructions.\n- **`index.html`**: A playable page.\n- **`my-3d2dge.js`**: The full engine, in one readable file with no dependencies.\n- **`my-3d2dge-agent.js`**: The smaller \"essential\" engine edition, with its own shorter manual.\n- **`package.json`**: Pins Playwright for the tools.\n\n## What the handoff expects\n\n- **Start from a kit or the template.** The README says: \"Start from a game, not an empty file.\"\n- **Read `API.md` first**, then search `API-INDEX.md` by name (for example, `grep -n \"ledge\\|landing\" API-INDEX.md`).\n- **Don't build standard features yourself.** The README says not to build \"a pause menu, settings, key rebinding, full screen or a loading screen.\" Configure them with `new E.Game({ menu: ... })`, and put heavy setup in `game.start({ boot: { steps } })`.\n- **Objects:** Read `docs/ASSETS.md` before drawing objects. For example, `r.shape('barrel', x, y, 0, { facing })` draws a core-library object in every view.\n- **Asset store:** A game loads a set with `E.addAssets(window.ASSETS['kenney-furniture'])`.\n\n## Bundling into a single HTML file\n\nThe README says: \"To hand a single file to a person, inline the two scripts into the page.\" It doesn't give further steps.\n\n## Run and test commands (verbatim from the README)\n\n```\nnpm install                          # Playwright, for the tools (once)\nnpx playwright install chromium      # its browser (once)\nnode game/test.mjs                   # the game's headless test: no browser, a second\nnode tools/check.mjs game/index.html # play it in a browser: errors, screenshots, its snapshot\n```\n\nThe README also says: \"Open `game/index.html` in a browser to play (or serve the folder: `npx serve`).\"\n\n## Headless testing\n\n`new E.Game()` runs headless in Node. `game.advance(seconds)` runs fixed 1/120 s steps, and `game.input.tap('jump')` simulates input. The README says the same inputs produce the same game on every machine.\n\n## Native perspectives\n\nThe README mentions views but doesn't list them. It says the docs define what x, y, and z mean \"in each view,\" and that 3D games are built on the 2D engine's views. The 3D engine (three.js and Rapier) is not included here.\n\n## Not covered in the content\n\n- **Art style:** The README only mentions \"the look to aim for,\" which is covered in `AI_GUIDE.md`. It doesn't describe the style.\n- **Animation:** Not described.\n- **Audio:** Only mentioned in passing as part of the kits (\"sound, touch controls\"). No audio system is described.","durationMs":9966,"url":"https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015vEC7dfPjT4tdeAnfxybaX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
